  [1mThe race I flagged did happen, and it was worse than I predicted.[0m I told you path-scoped [38;5;153mgit add[39m would keep the agents
  off each other. That was wrong: [38;5;153mgit commit[39m commits the whole index, not the paths you staged, so the slugify agent's
  commit swallowed truncate's two staged files. The slugify agent caught it and rebuilt its commit through a temporary
  index with a compare-and-swap ref update — no working-tree reset, no stash, and truncate's files were left staged and
  intact. I then verified the state myself and sent the truncate agent back in to commit its own piece, which it did as
  an ordinary commit on top.

  Net effect: each helper still landed as its own commit by its own agent, as you asked, and no history that anything
  else depended on was rewritten. But the recovery was luck plus a careful subagent, not the design. Two agents sharing
  one git index is not actually parallelizable at the commit step — the correct shape is agents that only write files,
  with a single serialized committer afterward. Worth knowing before the next fan-out.

